    "selected_growth_rate": 4.0,
    "growth_rate_source": "judgment: forward nominal revenue growth (core pricing above rail inflation + low-single-digit volume); equity CAGR rejected",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR is not usable: book equity fell from $21.2B (FY2014) to $12.2B (FY2022) on ~$45B of buybacks, then rebuilt as buybacks slowed, so the 3y equity CAGR (14.9%) and 5y (1.7%) describe capital return, not compounding. 5y revenue/EPS CAGRs start at the FY2020 COVID trough. 10y revenue CAGR is only 1.2% (coal decline offset pricing).",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF CAGR is slightly below reported FCF CAGR (SBC grew from $73M to $142M FY20-25) but SBC is <0.6% of revenue, so the gap is immaterial. EPS growth over the decade came mainly from margin (OR ~63% -> ~60%) and a ~32% share-count reduction, not from revenue."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Standalone UNP keeps doing what it has done for a decade: core pricing a little above rail cost inflation, volumes flat to low-single-digit as bulk (grain, renewable feedstocks) and domestic-intermodal truck conversion offset tariff-hit international intermodal and soft autos, and PSR discipline under Vena grinding the operating ratio from ~60% toward ~58.5%. Capex stays ~14.5% of revenue, the bonus-depreciation cash-tax holiday unwinds to ~22%, and revenue compounds ~3.5%. The NS merger is treated as outside the model: neither the synergies nor the leverage.",
    "bull": "Durable duopoly pricing plus a truck-to-rail conversion cycle (tight truck capacity, UP's service metrics at records) lift volume growth to 2-3% on top of pricing, and further PSR/automation cost-out (fuel efficiency, terminal automation, asset velocity) takes the OR toward ~55.5%, at or past the best in the industry. Capex intensity drifts down as velocity gains free locomotives and cars. Revenue compounds ~5% early, 3.5% terminal.",
    "bear": "A tariff-driven freight recession in 2027 (international intermodal, autos, chemicals) drops revenue ~1% and the OR back above 62% as volume deleverage meets a unionized cost base and a two-person-crew floor on headcount savings. Recovery is slow; captive-shipper rate scrutiny and post-merger-fight regulatory hostility (STB, DOJ, labor) cap pricing, so growth settles at 2.5% with margins ~3pp below today and capex stickier at 15%. Merger distraction or a failed deal (with a reverse termination fee) adds to the drag."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "UNP is a mature, irreplaceable network with pricing power and a stable ~50% EBITDA margin, valued on standalone unlevered owner FCF at an 8.25% USD WACC. Value is driven by the margin path (OR) and nominal pricing, not volume. The pending Norfolk Southern merger is deliberately excluded; it is a binary catalyst that would change leverage, share count and the growth profile.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "NS merger: if approved, ~225M new shares, ~$20B+ new debt plus assumed NS debt and multi-year integration risk (UP/SP 1996-98 service meltdown precedent) supersede this standalone model; if rejected, a reverse termination fee and lost management time",
      "Freight recession / tariffs: international intermodal and autos already down; volume deleverage moves the OR quickly",
      "Regulatory/labor: two-person crew rule, captive-shipper rate cases, post-East Palestine safety politics limit cost-out",
      "Cash-tax normalisation: the bonus-depreciation deferral is assumed to unwind to ~22%; a permanent lower cash rate would add value",
      "Terminal value is ~70%+ of EV; each 50bp of WACC moves value ~8-10%"
    ]
  },
